Free the texture cache's surfaces when a state save or load clears it

RasterizerCache::ClearAll, run by every state save and load
(System::serialize), emptied the page table, the cached pages and the dirty
regions, but never unregistered the surfaces they held: they stayed in
slot_surfaces with their images, reachable by nothing, never sentenced, so the
garbage collector never freed them. Upstream a load destroyed the whole GPU
right after, which bounded the leak to the saves; with the GPU kept across a
load (0016) and a rewind snapshot every few seconds (0014, 0015), the cache
grew by a texture cache's worth at each one, for the whole session: Majora's
Mask 3D on the host, 75 -> 1546 live surfaces over 17 snapshots.

Now ClearAll unregisters each surface it drops, as UnregisterAll does (each one
sentenced, freed by the per-frame garbage collector once the GPU is done with
it), and sentences the cubes left in the texture cube cache, whose faces it
dropped. Not from the destructor, where the surfaces go with the cache.

Measured (Tools/3ds-bench rewind, Majora's Mask 3D, 17 snapshots): live
surfaces stay between 80 and 132, the scene's own. roundtrip (Craftus, MM3D)
and rewindbridge (Craftus, MM3D) give the same results as before.

